<<~ ahu slot=`#/model-tiers-measured-fable-wrights-against-the-s`>>

! Model tiers, measured ~ Fable Wrights against the setups before them

Tier-Scout: Lares (Tasked Spirit). Read-only pass, 2026-10-30 UTC (operator's day 2026-10-29 PDT).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/method`>>

!! Method

* ''Claude seats.'' 116 subagent transcripts in the session's `subagents/` directory. The brief said about 230 files; that count includes the 116 `.meta.json` files beside them. My own transcript (`a688ffa1d…`) is left out, which leaves 115 seats.
** The model is read from each assistant message's `message.model`. Every seat ran one model.
** Usage is summed once per API message id. Claude Code writes one line per content block and repeats the usage on each line; the last copy is the final one.
** Scripts: `measure.py` → `agg.py` → `tables.py` and `cache.py`, all in this directory. Raw rows are in `data/rows2.json`.
* ''Eras.'' "fable-day" means a seat started at or after 2026-10-29 07:00Z (29 seats); "prior" means 2026-10-17 to 2026-10-29 (86 seats). The fable-day era is not Fable-only: it also holds the Opus Hive-Wright, the Sonnet Porch, Relay, Test and Vector Wrights, and the Opus scouts, readers and clerks. That lets same-day tiers compare against each other.
* ''Roles'' come from the seat's launch description (`meta.json`), checked against its first user message:
** ''lead:'' a `*-Wright` lane;
** ''reader:'' read, cold-read, crucible or Aftermath seats;
** ''scout:'' research, map, measure, collide or audit seats;
** ''helper:'' test migration, artifact seats, ink clerks, true-up and contract consolidation.
* ''Every named lead's model was confirmed from its transcript.'' Fable 5.1 (`claude-fable-5-1`): Walk, Mirror, Founding, Spec-Crucible. Opus 5.5 (`claude-opus-5-5`): Hive, Realm-Scout, Spec-Scout, True-Hand, Contract-Clerk. Sonnet 5.5 (`claude-sonnet-5-5`): Relay, Porch, Test. The 4 "Collide …" helpers under Spec-Crucible ran Fable too.
* ''Prices.'' API-eq $ uses the Anthropic table (SOURCED, https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing, fetched 2026-10-29). Columns are input / 5m write / 1h write / cache hit / output, in USD per million tokens:

| Model | Input | 5m write | 1h write | Cache hit | Output |h
| Fable 5.1 | 10 | 12.50 | 20 | 0.25 (0.025× input) | 50 |
| Opus 5.5 | 4 | 5 | 8 | 0.20 (0.05× input) | 20 |
| Sonnet 5.5 | 2 | 2.50 | 4 | 0.10 (0.05× input) | 10 |

Codex prices use the OpenAI table (SOURCED, https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing, fetched 2026-10-29). Columns are input / cached / output, in USD per million tokens, short context:

| Model | Input | Cached | Output |h
| `gpt-6.1-sol` | 2 | 0.10 | 10 |
| `gpt-6.1-terra` | 1 | 0.05 | 5 |
| `gpt-6-luna` | 0.10 | 0.01 | 0.50 |

API-eq $ weighs seats against each other. It is ''not'' what the Max or Codex plan charges. Max meters a five-hour window, a weekly window and a ''separate weekly Fable limit'' (SOURCED, https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11145838, fetched 2026-10-29).
* ''Prose against action.'' Visible output is estimated as (text chars + tool-input JSON chars + visible thinking chars) / 4.
** Every thinking block in these transcripts carries no visible text: adaptive thinking was always on, and the text is not kept. So "hidden-think est %" means the output tokens left over after the visible estimate.
** chars/4 is a rough tokenizer guess; JSON and code tokenize denser, so the hidden share is probably overstated by a few points (REASONED).
* ''Narration'' counts text blocks in every assistant message except the last (the hand-back). It is reported as chars per tool call and as the share of API calls that carry text and no tool.
* ''Commits'' count `git commit` commands seen in the seat's Bash calls (attempts included). The outcome table instead attributes real commits on the lead's lane branches by committer time inside the lead's window.

