Every tier works mostly through Bash: git, the battery, codex-seat launches, greps and heredocs. Edit and Write stay rare everywhere. The Fable leads were the only leads that edited files by hand in any number (8 Edit and 14 Write files across 3 seats). The briefs had told them to author the reds themselves.

!!! Reading the prose-against-action question

* ''Narration is small at every tier.''
** Visible text is under 3% of output tokens for every group. For the Fable leads it is about 6k tokens of 768k (0.8%).
** Most output spend goes to hidden adaptive thinking, about 58–69% of output tokens, and to tool-call inputs, about 30–35%.
** So "talk-story to itself" mostly happens in thinking you never see. It is not spoken text between tool calls.
* ''Fable narrates more per action than the other tiers'', but the volume is still small:
** visible text between tool calls: Fable 22 chars per call, Opus 10, prior Sonnet 13, today's Sonnet 3;
** text-only turns: 8.7% of Fable lead API calls, against 0.5% for today's Sonnet leads and 9.1% for the Opus Hive-Wright.
** Most text-only turns are wake hand-backs: a lead hands back to the controller once its seat launches, and wakes again on SendMessage.
* ''Fable batches its tool calls.''
** Fable leads ran 1.52 tool calls per API call, against 0.94–1.01 for Opus and Sonnet.
** The four Fable collide scouts ran 6.48 per call, firing parallel WebFetch and WebSearch.
** This is the clearest behavioural signature in the data. Fewer round trips mean fewer cache re-reads per unit of work.
* ''Hand-backs.''
** Fable leads ran shortest (median 225 words).
** The Opus readers ran longest (median 1,993 words; their briefs asked for whole reports).
** The Sonnet leads sat between, at 483 words today and 737 before.

